// kyoten::frame::Cursor — the cursor's mode a game asks for a player (the first slice's step 8, its sixth part, KYR-54;
// design/input.md § Decided, 2026-11-26, the sixth part's question 2). It is Output's own state, `"output": true` in the
// frame tier's records, as `TextEntry` is: the game requests and releases the lock whenever it likes, an Update processor
// changing the game's own state (a menu open, a pause) and an Output processor writing the mode from it. A game's prefab
// lists it on whatever entity asks: `"Cursor": { "player": 1, "mode": "Locked" }`.
//
// The platform reads every one after each frame, a player's strongest ask winning (`Locked`, then `Hidden`, then
// `Normal`, which asks nothing), and sets the window's cursor as it changes. While the game still asks `Locked` and the
// window dropped the lock by itself (Escape or a tab switch on the web, a focus loss natively), the engine asks again: the
// web locks at the player's next click, natively when the window has the focus again.

export { Cursor, CursorMode };

// A cursor's mode: `Normal`, shown and free; `Hidden`, free but not drawn over the window; `Locked`, hidden and held at
// the window's centre, its motion raw and unbounded (mouse look). Read and written by its case names.
@generate(Serializable, Deserializable)
type enum CursorMode { Normal, Hidden, Locked }

@generate(Serializable, Deserializable)
type value Cursor {
    // The player it is for, numbered from 1; 0 names none, and asks nothing.
    @field public int32 player = 0;
    // The mode that player's cursor is asked to be in.
    @field public CursorMode mode = CursorMode::Normal;

    // Naming no player, and asking `Normal`.
    public default ctor make() { }
}
